<!-- ============ TRUST PILLARS ============ -->
<section class="section" id="pillars">
  <div class="marker">04 // WHY YOU CAN TRUST IT</div>
  <h2 class="stitle">
    Three independent checks. <span class="blue">Hardware.</span> Signatures. <span class="dim">Open source.</span>
  </h2>
  <p class="ssub">
    <strong>Hardware</strong> locks down what software is running.
    <strong>Signatures</strong> prove every response came from that software.
    <strong>Open source</strong> lets anyone verify that the software itself isn't
    malicious. No single point you have to trust.
  </p>

  <!-- ===== CHAIN-OF-TRUST VISUALIZATION ===== -->
  <div class="trustchain">
    <div class="tc-rail">
      <div class="tc-nodes">

        <!-- LINK 1: HARDWARE — Intel TDX silicon enclave -->
        <div class="tc-node">
          <div class="tc-ix">PILLAR / 01 · HARDWARE</div>
          <div class="tc-input">
            <span class="label">Receives</span>
            <span class="value in">the software running on the node</span>
            <span class="arrow">↓</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tc-seal">
            <div class="glyph"><span class="big">TDX</span>HARDWARE<br/>LOCKED</div>
          </div>
          <div class="tc-vendor">Intel TDX</div>
          <div class="tc-role">Silicon root · sealed memory</div>
          <div class="tc-output">
            <div class="label">→ EMITS</div>
            <div class="credential">a hardware fingerprint of exactly what software is running</div>
            <div class="signedby"><span class="k">signed_by:</span> Intel root CA</div>
          </div>
          <div class="tc-body">Intel's chip seals the node and signs a fingerprint of what's running. Nothing outside the enclave can change it: not the host OS, not the hypervisor, not the cloud admin.</div>
        </div>

        <!-- LINK 2: CRYPTOGRAPHY — per-node key, attested binding, signed RPC -->
        <div class="tc-node">
          <div class="tc-ix">PILLAR / 02 · SIGNATURES</div>
          <div class="tc-input">
            <span class="label">Receives</span>
            <span class="value">hardware fingerprint + unique signing key</span>
            <span class="arrow">↓</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tc-seal">
            <div class="glyph"><span class="big">SIG</span>EVERY<br/>RESPONSE</div>
          </div>
          <div class="tc-vendor">Per-node key</div>
          <div class="tc-role">signing key sealed in hardware</div>
          <div class="tc-output">
            <div class="label">→ EMITS</div>
            <div class="credential">every response signed by the node's key, with hardware confirming the software is correct</div>
            <div class="signedby"><span class="k">signed_by:</span> enclave key + cloud key</div>
          </div>
          <div class="tc-body">The enclave generates a fresh signing key that never leaves the hardware. The cloud signs an attestation binding the key to the measured image. Every response is signed by the enclave's key.</div>
        </div>

        <!-- LINK 3: PUBLIC VERIFIABILITY — reproducible builds + public source -->
        <div class="tc-node">
          <div class="tc-ix">PILLAR / 03 · PUBLIC VERIFIABILITY</div>
          <div class="tc-input">
            <span class="label">Receives</span>
            <span class="value">public source code → deterministic software build</span>
            <span class="arrow">↓</span>
          </div>
          <div class="tc-seal">
            <div class="glyph"><span class="big">SRC</span>OPEN<br/>SOURCE</div>
          </div>
          <div class="tc-vendor">Open source</div>
          <div class="tc-role">Public code · anyone can audit</div>
          <div class="tc-output">
            <div class="label">→ PROVES</div>
            <div class="credential">anyone can rebuild from source and confirm the running software matches the public code</div>
            <div class="signedby"><span class="k">verified_by:</span> independent rebuilders</div>
          </div>
          <div class="tc-body">The node's code is open source and built deterministically. Anyone can rebuild from source and confirm the on-chain image is what the public code compiles to.</div>
        </div>

      </div>
      <!-- chain link bridges sitting on the rail -->
      <div class="tc-link l1">⧉</div>
      <div class="tc-link l2">⧉</div>
    </div>

    <!-- final verification badge -->
    <div class="tc-verify">
      <div>
        <div class="vlbl">→ YOU VERIFY</div>
        <div class="vh">Three independent checks, one guarantee.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="veq">
        <code>σ → <span style="color:var(--accent)">I_pub_n</span> → <span style="color:var(--accent)">A_live</span>.D_image ∈ <span style="color:var(--accent)">Registry</span></code>
      </div>
      <div class="vbadge">VERIFIED</div>
    </div>

  </div>

</section>

